# Frozen single-owner performance admission under the corrected policy

**Decision (22 October 2026).** Preserve the correct `dec7509b` variant and its measured gains. Performance parity is **unproved** against both same-machine main (`4e25a96a`) and published 0.1.0 across the fixed seven families, so stop-at-parity has not been reached and the draft remains held. Do not reject the whole variant because of the contaminated pooled 512-label p95, impose a 5% floor, demand a parity-sized deletion before allowing a small coherent improvement, or infer an intrinsic tail cause. This is a read-only interpretation of the frozen wave, not a new admission run, product choice, merge, or release decision.

The authenticated 145-identity candidate capture was reused byte-for-byte in separate same-machine comparisons with each control. Both dense operations were included in both comparisons. Main had five clock-confounded comparison skips; published had nine and its two documented unavailable outline identities. Skipped/unavailable required identities cannot pass admission by inference. All raw samples, excluded identities and adverse tails remain in the receipts; no arm receives a favorable tail deletion. The Node matrix measures complete package operations, not GPU time or displayed frame pacing.

## Ordinary regime, with tails kept visible

Values are complete-operation pooled nearest-rank **p50 / p90 ms**. Block ranges are medians of the eight fresh-process blocks, not reconstructed per-block p90s. Candidate and controls were run under the same original single-call policy, though their separate runs are not paired callbacks.

| Family and operation | Main p50 / p90 | Published p50 / p90 | Candidate p50 / p90 | Block evidence and reading |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |
| 1 — long unsafe-boundary reflow | 0.423 / 0.601 | 0.542 / 0.704 | 0.984 / 1.382 | Candidate's eight medians 0.794–1.190, above main's 0.407–0.452 and published's 0.534–0.549. Ordinary loss. |
| 2 — rewrite 512 labels in one group | 7.602 / 8.625 | 8.143 / 9.586 | 10.295 / 77.719 | Candidate has one 113.293 block; its other seven medians are 9.191–12.660, versus main 7.466–8.160 and published 7.989–10.557. An ordinary median deficit remains, but the pooled p90 is also polluted by the high block. |
| 2 — publish 1,024 retained Text instances | 16.547 / 17.895 | 16.686 / 18.097 | 19.548 / 21.883 | All candidate block medians 18.376–19.977, versus main 15.913–17.454 and published 16.061–16.865. Loss independent of the 512 spike. |
| 3 — cold mount and publish 22k glyphs | 18.209 / 18.915 | 19.349 / 20.284 | 21.121 / 22.807 | Candidate blocks 20.648–21.468; control blocks stay below 19.650. Ordinary cold loss. |
| 3 — measurement after width change | 0.918 / 1.113 | 0.988 / 1.168 | 1.359 / 3.656 | Seven candidate blocks are 1.246–1.551, above all main (0.894–0.939) and published (0.953–1.025) blocks; an additional candidate block is 4.858. Ordinary loss plus separate elevated block. |
| 4 — one-label first set | 0.036 / 0.050 | 0.041 / 0.073 | 0.157 / 0.238 | Candidate blocks 0.131–0.208, versus main 0.034–0.038 and published 0.033–0.054. Clear small-ingress loss; the 1,000-label first-set row has the same direction. |
| 5 — balanced sparse scene publication, 1,000 labels | 5.247 / 5.894 | 5.137 / 5.557 | 3.592 / 4.969 | Candidate blocks 3.266–4.354, below every corresponding control block. Retained gain. |
| 6 — edit and independently copy one among 1,000 labels | 3.228 / 3.930 | 5.937 / 6.963 | 2.138 / 2.571 | Candidate blocks 1.967–2.317, below both controls. Retained gain. |

The long unsafe-boundary **text edit** also has an ordinary candidate block regime of 1.866–2.208 ms (seven blocks), above main's 1.674–1.717 and published's 1.740–1.805, with a separate 8.071 ms candidate block. Other retained gains include 1,000 labels with 100 immediate edits, measurements and publication (p50/p90 candidate 19.859/39.810, main 100.176/136.880, published 163.723/176.003 ms); interleaved edit-read publication (49.777/67.785, 135.269/160.242, 193.260/203.944); scattered 8,192-span setter and publication (15.735/16.878, 21.201/22.275, 43.506/45.927); and per-glyph inspection after width change (4.864/7.254, 7.429/10.002, 9.545/10.975). These gains are real observations under the saved protocol and do not compensate for another fixed family's loss.

Family 7 remains mixed/open. The candidate 512 pooled p95 is 117.499 ms, with 13 of 128 retained samples above 50 ms and one 113.293 ms block median. That is a recorded elapsed-time regime, not one stray outlier, but the saved sorted samples cannot identify its cause. The symmetric rule applies to broad control tails too: main's first 1,000-label balanced engine-publication block is 48.081 ms versus its ordinary ~4.7–5.2 ms blocks, and the browser published WebGPU control has late high blocks. Keep every tail and report p50, p90 and blocks together; do not let any pooled tail alone adjudicate a correct implementation.

## What the warmup and browser evidence change

The original Labs 0.9.0 `benchFn` gives these slow callbacks one untimed call per fresh child because its two extra calls are conditional on the first completing within 0.5 ms. The bounded, instrumented 512 diagnostic used the unchanged frozen callback with eight alternating pairs. All eight 20-extra-untimed-call block medians beat the stock arm by 0.655–1.388 ms; stock retained p50/p90 was 9.463/10.635 ms, warmed 8.492/8.878 ms. No call or raw sample in either arm exceeded 50 ms. This establishes ordinary warmup sensitivity **in that diagnostic sitting**. It neither explains the saved 113 ms block nor authorizes replacing its samples. The trace changed pilot/stop behavior and added instrumentation, so it is not an admission comparison against main or published.

Consequently the old one-call matrix remains an honest measurement of that protocol, including the stable ordinal separations above. Its exact percentages, tail magnitudes and strong product-causality claims should not be treated as final. The candidate and both controls need the **same** explicit, fixed, untimed warmup policy in any future matched admission; the diagnostic's 20 calls are evidence for testing that policy, not a universally calibrated count. A symmetric policy may move all arms. It cannot be assumed to erase the ordinary candidate deficits or gains. The prior p95-based rejection criterion in `matrix-admission-review.md` is superseded by this corrected interpretation; its measurements and exclusions remain preserved.

The matched Chromium probe on a different flat fixture shows much smaller complete CPU gaps to main: WebGPU 512/1,024 candidate p50/p90 6.6/7.2 and 13.2/14.0 ms versus main 6.4/7.0 and 12.8/13.8; WebGL2 candidate 6.8/7.2 and 13.2/13.9 versus main 6.2/9.5 and 12.4/20.4. Published WebGPU has late high blocks. This supports a fixture/runtime-dependent boundary observation, not Node family parity. The browser fixture differs in font size, wrapping, width, hierarchy, capacity and timed inspection/traversal; its CPU render-return timing is not GPU completion or frame presentation. Output/readback proofs passed, while the reported line glyph-count difference remains explicit.

Memory admission uses exact Glyph-owned allocations only. Candidate requested Rust shaper allocation counts exist for named workloads; a byte-identical feature-off main diagnostic and paired main/published counts are not yet established. Exact Glyph-owned JavaScript and GPU buffer accounts are absent. RSS, JS heap and whole-process/browser totals cannot fill that gap. Shipping shaper/JavaScript size increases remain review evidence, not a latency or memory offset. Ongoing editorial and main allocator work is outside this review.

**Smallest defensible completion path.** The sole executor should finish a uniform, fixed untimed warmup rule for the exact Labs single-call path, verify fixture state and measurement symmetry, then make one authenticated, same-machine fixed-seven-family comparison with one candidate capture reused against main and published 0.1.0. Keep original and new distributions separate, including all tails and exclusions. If the ordinary losses persist, choose a bounded source change from an evidenced cost on those operations, preserve the correct immediate-read and publication contract and existing gains, and admit it only through the same fixed-family comparison and correctness/delivery checks. Small coherent improvements are eligible; there is no 5% floor or requirement to identify one parity-sized deletion before trying them. Stop at verified parity or better, with no subsequent boost wave. This review does not pick a speculative architecture, authorize a new product wave on its own, or claim that another matrix has already passed.

Evidence: `matrix-candidate/{manifest.json,candidate.json}`; `matrix-main-final/{manifest.json,baseline.json,candidate.json}`; `matrix-published-final/{manifest.json,baseline.json,candidate.json}`; `candidate-distribution-results.json`, `main-distribution-results.json`, `published-distribution-results.json`; `dense-tail-analysis.json`; `labs-spike-diagnostic-final/receipt.json`; `labs-spike-interpretation.md`; `dense-browser/{candidate.json,main-final.json,published.json,dense-browser-comparison.json}`; `browser-boundary-decision.md`; `freeze.json`; held-single-owner report. No benchmark, build, gate or product edit was run for this review.
